{"compatibility":"minor","safety_note_md":"# SafeStan Safety Note

Compatibility: minor.

Protected data: `Y1` and `Y2` (the two rows of the original transformed-data observation `Y`).

Generated files: `model_safe.stan`, `data_safe.json`.

Changes: lifted the deterministic transformed-data observations to top-level protected data variables: `Y1[i] = 1` and `Y2[i] = 0` for `i = 1, ..., I`, exactly matching the original assignments to `Y[1, i]` and `Y[2, i]`. Replaced the looped indexed likelihood `Y[j] ~ poisson_log(...)` with two vectorized built-in sampling statements whose left sides are plain protected identifiers. The predictor `est` and the priors on `beta` and `beta0` are unchanged. The generated data omits the supplied extra `J` field because `J` was a transformed-data constant, not a declared top-level data input.
","model_safe_stan":"// Endo: conditional inference in case-contrl studies 
// http://www.openbugs.net/Examples/Endo.html

// In this example, three methods of different 
// model specifications are used for one equivalent
// model. This is method 3. 

data {
  int n10;
  int n01;
  int n11;
  int I;
  array[I] int<lower=0> Y1;
  array[I] int<lower=0> Y2;
}
transformed data {
  array[2] vector<lower=0>[I] est;
  for (i in 1 : n10) {
    est[1, i] = 1;
    est[2, i] = 0;
  }
  for (i in (n10 + 1) : (n10 + n01)) {
    est[1, i] = 0;
    est[2, i] = 1;
  }
  for (i in (n10 + n01 + 1) : (n10 + n01 + n11)) {
    est[1, i] = 1;
    est[2, i] = 1;
  }
  for (i in (n10 + n01 + n11 + 1) : I) {
    est[1, i] = 0;
    est[2, i] = 0;
  }
}
parameters {
  real beta;
  vector[I] beta0;
}
model {
  // METHOD 3 fit standard Poisson regressions relative to baseline
  beta ~ normal(0, 1000);
  beta0 ~ normal(0, 1000);
  Y1 ~ poisson_log(beta0 + beta * est[1]);
  Y2 ~ poisson_log(beta0 + beta * est[2]);
}
